vl53l7cx_is_alive() must not decide from uninitialised memory.

`device_id` and `revision_id` are plain locals, and the function issues all four of
its transfers unconditionally -- it ORs their statuses and never returns early. The
Zephyr port does not clear the destination on a failed read: read_chunk() hands the
buffer straight to i2c_transfer() and returns its error, leaving the caller's bytes
untouched. So when the ID reads do not happen, or happen and fail, *p_is_alive is
computed by comparing whatever was on the stack against 0xF0 and 0x02.

WHY THAT IS NOT MERELY UNTIDY HERE. The boot-time recovery pass probes six addresses
in a loop, through the same call frame at the same depth. A sensor that really is
present leaves 0xF0 and 0x02 in those two slots; the next address in the loop, with
nobody on it and every transfer NACKed, can read its own answer out of the previous
one's leftovers and be reported alive. The pass would then issue a five-second stop
sequence to an empty address, and -- worse for what the pass is for -- the census it
produces is evidence, so a false positive argues for a hypothesis nobody tested.

It also makes `answered` trustworthy enough to classify on, which the adapter now
does: a cold boot gives a clean NACK with nothing acknowledged, while a survivor that
NACKs one transfer and still answers the ID reads gives a NACK with an answer. Those
are different observations and the adapter has to tell them apart. It cannot, if the
value it reads was never written.

Initialising to zero rather than to a sentinel: zero is not 0xF0, so an unanswered
probe reports not-alive, which is the same conclusion the uninitialised version
reached by luck on nearly every boot.

Applied to the copy in the build directory, never in place, so SHA256SUMS continues
to answer whether upstream changed.
diff --git a/modules/vl53l7cx_api.c b/modules/vl53l7cx_api.c
--- a/modules/vl53l7cx_api.c
+++ b/modules/vl53l7cx_api.c
@@ -219,7 +219,7 @@
 		uint8_t				*p_is_alive)
 {
 	uint8_t status = VL53L7CX_STATUS_OK;
-	uint8_t device_id, revision_id;
+	uint8_t device_id = 0, revision_id = 0;
 
 	status |= VL53L7CX_WrByte(&(p_dev->platform), 0x7fff, 0x00);
 	status |= VL53L7CX_RdByte(&(p_dev->platform), 0, &device_id);
